Proportion of top 100 podcasts that are hosted by men, according to a recen=
t study by researchers at USC Annenberg=E2=80=99s Inclusion Initiative, per=
 (https://www.npr.org/2025/11/11/nx-s1-5605983/bros-really-are-dominating-p=
odcasting?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-9x6kixPR_v7-G0HZISyaw-GYqSlaynE5d9b8pjluiWi5Fqam=
5MLgGGqxfnE_DEZ1mPegBD1DblCFR-946BWFvt2iE1Rg&_hsmi=3D391296211 ) NPR. The s=
tudy, which analyzed data from 600 of Spotify=E2=80=99s most popular pods o=
f 2024, found that ~75% of guests were also male, which might explain why =
=E2=80=9Cpodcast bros=E2=80=9D is a term but =E2=80=9Cpodcast chicks=E2=80=
=9D isn=E2=80=99t.

But women aren=E2=80=99t the only underrepresented group in the audio lands=
cape =E2=80=94 researchers revealed that among the top 100 podcasts, over 7=
7% of hosts were white, making the podcast industry=E2=80=99s racial and ge=
nder inequalities far greater than those found in TV, movies, and music.

